(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-tuberculosis-drugs-quiz"]={"title":"Tuberculosis Drugs Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In which situation is isoniazid used as a single drug?","choices":["Tuberculosis prophylaxis","Active tuberculosis","Meningococcal prophylaxis","Infective endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is used alone for prophylaxis; in active disease it is always part of combination therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which laboratory monitoring does pyrazinamide require?","choices":["Drug trough levels","Red-green color vision","Liver enzymes","Kidney function"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide causes hepatitis in about 15 percent of patients, so liver enzymes are monitored. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which first-line tuberculosis drug needs its dose adjusted in renal disease because about 75 percent is excreted unchanged?","choices":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About 75 percent of ethambutol is excreted unchanged, so its dose is adjusted in renal disease. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which patient factor raises the risk of isoniazid hepatitis?","choices":["Younger age","Renal disease","Hyperuricemia","Alcoholism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid hepatitis is increased in alcoholics and increases with age. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Rifampin interacts with anticoagulants, contraceptives, digoxin and theophylline through which action?","choices":["Induction of cytochrome P450 enzymes","Inhibition of cytochrome P450 enzymes","Inhibition of human RNA polymerase","Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes (CYP1A2, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4), which is how it interacts with anticoagulants, contraceptives, digoxin, ketoconazole and theophylline. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does ethambutol's weakening of the mycobacterial cell wall do for combination therapy?","choices":["It kills the bacteria outright","It shortens the treatment course","It lets other agents in more easily","It keeps resistance from forming"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol inhibits mycolic acid incorporation into membranes, and the weakened cell wall allows other agents better access. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which allergic reaction is listed for isoniazid?","choices":["Flu-like syndrome","Lupus-like syndrome","Peripheral neuritis","Hepatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid's allergic reactions include a lupus-like syndrome. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why do antimicrobials that act on cell division work less well against Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","choices":["It grows slowly and can lie dormant","It divides faster than drugs can act","It has no cell wall for drugs to target","It is a gram-negative rod"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is very slow growing and can exist in dormant states for long periods, so drugs that act on dividing cells work less well. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Rifampin's jaundice and hepatitis are a particular concern in which patients?","choices":["Chronic kidney disease","Pregnancy in any trimester","Chronic liver disease","Gout or high uric acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin can cause jaundice and hepatitis, a particular concern in chronic liver disease. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which four drugs make up the first two months of combination therapy for tuberculosis?","choices":["Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, vancomycin","Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, amikacin","Rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, doxycycline","Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The first two months combine isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol and pyrazinamide, followed by four months of isoniazid and rifampin. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does rifampin inhibit in Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","choices":["DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Mycolic acid production","Bacterial catalase-peroxidase","Cytochrome P450 enzymes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin inhibits the bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase by forming a complex with the enzyme, with no effect on the human enzyme. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient notices orange tears and orange urine after starting tuberculosis therapy. Which drug explains this?","choices":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin colors secretions orange: tears, sweat and urine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does pyrazinamide add to combined tuberculosis therapy?","choices":["It prevents peripheral neuritis","It weakens the cell wall","It induces liver enzymes","It shortens the treatment course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide decreases the duration of combined therapy; it is given during the first two months only. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Isoniazid's half-life varies widely between patients (about 70 minutes versus 2.5 hours). What explains the difference?","choices":["Renal function","Bacterial catalase-peroxidase","Cytochrome P450 induction","Fast or slow acetylation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most isoniazid (75 to 95 percent) is excreted as an acetylated metabolite, and fast acetylators clear it in about 70 minutes against 2.5 hours in slow acetylators. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"After the first two months of tuberculosis treatment, which two drugs continue for the next four months?","choices":["Ethambutol and pyrazinamide","Isoniazid and rifampin","Isoniazid and ethambutol","Rifampin and pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After two months of four drugs, isoniazid and rifampin continue for four more months. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits uric acid excretion, so that nearly all patients taking it become hyperuricemic?","choices":["Isoniazid","Pyrazinamide","Rifampin","Vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide inhibits uric acid excretion, and all patients taking it are hyperuricemic. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
